{"cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-new-england","n":"Cuisine of New England","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in United States of America","intro":[["The cuisine of New England is an American cuisine which originated in the New England region of the United States, and traces its roots to traditional English cuisine and Native American cuisine of the Abenaki, Narragansett, Niantic, Wabanaki, Wampanoag, and other native peoples. It also includes influences from Irish, French-Canadian, Italian, and Portuguese cuisine, among others. It is characterized by extensive use of potatoes, beans, dairy products, and seafood, resulting from its historical reliance on its seaports and fishing industry. Corn, the major crop historically grown by Native American tribes in New England, continues to be grown in all New England states, primarily as sweet corn although flint corn is grown as well. It is traditionally used in hasty puddings, cornbreads and corn chowders."],["Many of New England's earliest Puritan settlers were from eastern England, where baking foods (for instance, pies, beans, and turkey) was more common than frying, as was the tradition elsewhere."],["Three prominent characteristic foodstuffs native to New England are maple syrup, cranberries and blueberries. The traditional standard starch is potato. Traditional New England cuisine is known for a lack of strong spices, largely due to local 19th-century health reformers, most prominently Sylvester Graham, who advocated eating bland food. Ground black pepper, parsley, garlic, and sage are common, with a few Caribbean additions, such as nutmeg, plus several Italian spices."],["The use of cream is common because it relies on dairy. The favored cooking techniques are stewing, steaming, and baking. Many local ingredients, such as squash, corn and local beans, sunflowers, wild turkey, maple syrup, cranberries and dishes such as cornbread, Johnnycakes and Indian pudding were adopted from Native American cuisine."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-new-england"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_New_England"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Yankee Magazine: Chowder Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newengland.com","note":"Recipes from Yankee, the long-running New England regional magazine.","url":"https://newengland.com/food/soups-stews-chowders/favorite-chowder-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"magazine"}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The New England Clam Shack Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Brooke Dojny","note":"Recipes from New England clam shacks, lobster pounds, and chowder houses.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781580174732","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781580174732"},{"name":"The New England Cookbook: 350 Recipes from Town and Country, Land and Sea, Hearth and Home","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Brooke Dojny","note":"Broad collection of New England home cooking.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781558321397","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781558321397"}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":5,"links":[["Beach pizza","dish/beach-pizza"],["Boston baked beans","dish/boston-baked-beans"],["Clam cake","dish/clam-cake"],["Parker House roll","dish/parker-house-roll"],["Thanksgiving sandwich","dish/thanksgiving-sandwich"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Navy beans","ingredient/navy-beans"],["Clam","ingredient/clam"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Margarine","ingredient/margarine"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Turkey as food","ingredient/turkey-as-food"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Tripoli%27s_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg/500px-Tripoli%27s_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"uzi978","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tripoli's_in_Salisbury%2C_Massachusetts.jpg","of":"Beach pizza"}},"dish/ajoblanco":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/ajoblanco","n":"Ajoblanco","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Andalusian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Ajoblanco (sometimes written ajo blanco) is a popular Spanish cold soup typical from Granada and Málaga (Andalusia). This dish is made of bread, crushed almonds, garlic, water, olive oil, salt and sometimes vinegar. It is usually served with grapes or slices of melon. When almonds were not available, for instance during the post-war period, flour from dried beans was used."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-ajoblanco"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajoblanco"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Andalusian cuisine","cuisine/andalusian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Olive oil","ingredient/olive-oil"]]},{"h":"More from Andalusian cuisine","links":[["Chicharrón","dish/chicharron"],["Gachas","dish/gachas"],["Gazpachuelo","dish/gazpachuelo"],["Migas","dish/migas"],["Porra antequerana","dish/porra-antequerana"],["Puchero","dish/puchero"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/db/Ajo_blanco.jpg/500px-Ajo_blanco.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/deed.en","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ajo_blanco.jpg"}},"ingredient/cumin":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cumin","n":"Cumin","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Cumin is a flowering plant in the family Apiaceae, native to the Irano-Turanian Region. Its seeds – each one contained within a fruit, which is dried – are used in the cuisines of many cultures in both whole and ground form. Although cumin is used in traditional medicine, there is no high-quality evidence that it is safe or effective as a therapeutic agent."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cumin"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumin"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Argentine cuisine","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",[["Salsa golf","dish/salsa-golf"]]],["Indian cuisine","cuisine/indian-cuisine",[["Baingan bharta","dish/baingan-bharta"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Anise, badian, coriander, cumin, caraway, fennel and juniper berries, raw","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/faostat/en/#data/QCL","world":2316018,"top":[["India","cuisine/indian-cuisine",1758472],["Mexico","cuisine/mexican-cuisine",121253],["Russia","cuisine/russian-cuisine",73227],["Syria","cuisine/syrian-cuisine",64853],["Iran","cuisine/iranian-cuisine",61545],["China","cuisine/chinese-cuisine",52433],["Egypt","cuisine/egyptian-cuisine",30383],["Morocco","cuisine/moroccan-cuisine",28286],["Turkey","cuisine/turkish-cuisine",26876],["Vietnam","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine",18143],["Afghanistan","cuisine/afghan-cuisine",17905],["Kenya","cuisine/kenyan-cuisine",14585],["Canada","cuisine/canadian-cuisine",12432],["Tunisia","cuisine/tunisian-cuisine",10737],["Argentina","cuisine/argentine-cuisine",7246]]}},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Brinjal","ingredient/brinjal"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Cilantro","ingredient/cilantro"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Garam masala","ingredient/garam-masala"],["Green peas","ingredient/green-peas"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Tomato","ingredient/tomato"],["Ketchup","ingredient/ketchup"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnai